===============================================================================
PART 1 : WHAT IT IS, AND WHY IT EXISTS
===============================================================================

Two or more methods in the same class share ONE name but take DIFFERENT
parameters.

    void sum(int a, int b)            { }
    void sum(double a, int b)         { }
    void sum(int a, int b, int c)     { }

WHY IT EXISTS

One logical operation should have ONE name, whatever you hand it. Without
overloading, every different input type would force a different method name:

        sumTwoInts(2, 4);
        sumIntAndDouble(2.4, 1);
        sumThreeInts(1, 2, 3);        // caller must memorise 3 names

    with overloading ->

        sum(2, 4);
        sum(2.4, 1);
        sum(1, 2, 3);                 // caller remembers ONE name

Four concrete reasons:

    1. READABILITY -- the name describes the INTENT, not the argument types.

    2. DEFAULT VALUES -- Java has no optional parameters. Overloading is how
       Java fakes them. See PART 6.

    3. API EVOLUTION -- adding an overload never breaks existing callers.
       That is how Java itself has grown for 30 years without breaking old
       programs.

    4. TYPE FLEXIBILITY -- the caller passes what it already has, instead of
       converting or casting first.

THE PROOF YOU HAVE ALREADY BEEN USING

    System.out.println() has TEN overloads:

        println(int)     println(long)    println(float)   println(double)
        println(boolean) println(char)    println(char[])  println(String)
        println(Object)  println()

    That is why it works on anything without you ever thinking about it.
    Other examples: Math.max (4), String.valueOf (9),
    StringBuilder.append (13), Arrays.sort (20+).

WHEN NOT TO OVERLOAD

Only overload when the methods do the SAME logical thing. Same name for
unrelated behaviour is confusing, not clever:

        void process(int userId)       // looks up a user
        void process(int retryCount)   // retries a network call   <- BAD IDEA

Two different operations deserve two different names.

    Overloading = for the CALLER's convenience.
    Overriding  = for EXTENSIBILITY.       (see MethodOverriding/Notes)


===============================================================================
PART 2 : THE RULES  (what makes a legal overload)
===============================================================================

    1. The method name must be the SAME.

    2. The parameter list MUST differ -- by type, by count, or by order.
       At least one of the three. This is the only thing that matters.

    3. Everything else is IGNORED by the compiler:
            return type, access modifier, thrown exceptions, method body,
            static or non-static, final or non-final, parameter names.
       None of them can create an overload on their own.

    4. Same class is normal, but a subclass works too. Inheritance is
       ALLOWED but not REQUIRED.

Short version:

        Same name + different parameter list = overload.
        Same name + same parameter list      = compile error.


===============================================================================
PART 3 : THE FEATURES  (how it behaves)
===============================================================================

    1. It is COMPILE-TIME polymorphism. The compiler decides which method
       to call. Also called static binding or early binding.

    2. The decision uses the DECLARED types of the arguments. The runtime
       object plays no part at all.

    3. It works WITHOUT inheritance. One class is enough.

    4. private, static, final and main can all be overloaded. (None of them
       can be overridden -- that is the difference.)

    5. CONSTRUCTORS can be overloaded, with exactly the same rules. You
       already did this in the Constructor package.

    6. Return types MAY differ -- but only if the parameter lists already
       differ. A different return type alone is never enough.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : THE THREE WAYS TO DIFFER
===============================================================================

The parameter list can differ in exactly three ways. Any one is enough.

  a) TYPE of the parameters

        void show(int a)
        void show(String a)
        void show(double a)

  b) NUMBER of the parameters

        void show(int a)
        void show(int a, int b)
        void show(int a, int b, int c)
        void show()                       // zero parameters is also a list

  c) SEQUENCE (order) of the parameter types

        void show(int a, String b)
        void show(String a, int b)        // same types, opposite order

     This one is legal but the rarest in real code. Usually it exists as a
     convenience door into the same method -- it should just reorder the
     arguments and delegate. See PART 6.

     NOTE: order only counts when the TYPES differ. These are the same
     signature and will not compile together:

        void show(int a, int b)
        void show(int b, int a)           // ERROR -- names do not matter


===============================================================================
PART 5 : HOW THE COMPILER PICKS  (resolution order)
===============================================================================

The compiler searches in this exact order and STOPS at the first phase that
finds a match:

    Phase 1 : Exact match           (no conversion needed at all)
    Phase 2 : Widening / promotion  (int -> long -> float -> double)
    Phase 3 : Autoboxing / unboxing (int -> Integer, Integer -> int)
    Phase 4 : Varargs               (int...)

Consequence: WIDENING always beats AUTOBOXING, and both beat VARARGS.

    void show(long a)     { }   // widening
    void show(Integer a)  { }   // autoboxing
    void show(int... a)   { }   // varargs

    show(10);   // calls show(long) -- widening wins

THE WIDENING (PROMOTION) PATH

    byte -> short -> int -> long -> float -> double
            char  -> int -> long -> float -> double

  * Java promotes automatically but NEVER narrows automatically.
    A double will not silently become an int.
  * char promotes to int, but char and short do not promote into each other
    (both are 16 bits, one signed and one unsigned).
  * boolean never participates in promotion.

AMBIGUITY IS A COMPILE ERROR

If two candidates are equally good, the compiler refuses to guess:

    void sum(double a, int b)  { }
    void sum(int a, double b)  { }

    sum(2, 4);      // error: reference to sum is ambiguous
                    // promoting either argument works equally well

Add an exact match -- sum(int, int) -- and Phase 1 resolves it instantly.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : THE DELEGATION PATTERN -- OVERLOADING AS DEFAULT VALUES
===============================================================================

This is the part that matters in real code. Learn it properly.

Java has NO default or optional parameters. Other languages write

        def order(size, quantity=1, cheese=False)      # Python

Java cannot. Instead, Java writes one short overload per default, and each
one fills in a missing value and passes the call along.

THE RULE

        ONE overload holds the formula. Every other overload supplies one
        missing value and delegates to the next one down.

DIRECTION -- information flows from LESS to MORE. Never upward.

        order()              --supplies "Medium"-->  order(size)
        order(size)          --supplies 1--------->  order(size, qty)
        order(qty, size)     --reorders----------->  order(size, qty)
        order(size, qty)     --supplies false----->  order(size, qty, cheese)
                                                                          *
                                                     * the formula lives here

Think of a form with three blanks. Each overload fills in one blank and hands
the form on. The last one, holding a complete form, finally does the work.

IN CODE

        double order() {
            return order("Medium");             // fills in the size
        }
        double order(String size) {
            return order(size, 1);              // fills in the quantity
        }
        double order(int quantity, String size) {
            return order(size, quantity);       // only reorders
        }
        double order(String size, int quantity) {
            return order(size, quantity, false);  // fills in the cheese flag
        }
        double order(String size, int quantity, boolean cheese) {
            ... the ONLY method that calculates anything ...
        }

WHY IT MATTERS

    * Change the price and you edit ONE line, not five.
    * The logic cannot drift out of sync between overloads.
    * Four of the five methods are one line and cannot contain a bug.

THE MISTAKE TO AVOID -- delegating the wrong way

        order(size, qty, cheese)  --calls-->  order()   just to fetch 250

    Here the method with the MOST information asks the method with the LEAST
    information for a number. That is not delegation -- it is a constant with
    a method wrapped around it. If the default size ever changes, the value
    is silently wrong.

    Ask yourself: does each overload ADD information before passing it on?
    If an overload is reaching DOWNWARD for a value, the chain is backwards.

SAME IDEA, DIFFERENT NAME

    Constructors do this too, using this(...) instead of the method name.
    That is constructor chaining -- see the Constructor package. Identical
    concept, identical direction rule.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : READING YOUR OWN CODE
===============================================================================

Sum.java -- every call is an EXACT match, so Phase 1 always wins:

    void sum(int a, int b)         -> s.sum(2, 4)
    void sum(double a, int b)      -> s.sum(2.4, 1)
    void sum(int a, double b)      -> s.sum(7, 3.5)
    void sum(int a, int b, int c)  -> s.sum(1, 2, 3)      (different count)

  Trap: delete sum(int, int), then still call s.sum(2, 4). The compiler can
  promote either the first argument or the second, neither candidate is more
  specific, and you get "reference to sum is ambiguous" at COMPILE time.


Test.java -- overloading across a parent and a child class:

    class SuperClass            { void msg(int x, int y)       }
    class Subclass extends ...  { void msg(double x, double y) }

    sc.msg(10, 20);   -> "Hello Java"                       (SuperClass method)
    sc.msg(2.5, 3);   -> "Welcome you in Java programming"  (Subclass method)

  Why does the first call run the PARENT method even though sc is a Subclass?
  Overload resolution considers every method visible on the reference type,
  inherited ones included. msg(10, 20) is an exact match for msg(int, int),
  so Phase 1 wins immediately.

  This proves overloading is decided by the COMPILER from declared types --
  the runtime object plays no part.


ShippingCharge.java -- all three ways to differ, plus delegation:

    calculate(double)                          number
    calculate(double, String)                  number
    calculate(String, double)                  sequence
    calculate(double, String, String)          number    <- holds the formula
    calculate(double, int)                     type

PizzaOrder.java -- the same shape, with a zero-arg overload and a boolean:

    order()                                    zero parameters
    order(String)                              number
    order(int, String)                         sequence
    order(String, int)                         number
    order(String, int, boolean)                type      <- holds the formula


===============================================================================
PART 8 : WHAT DOES *NOT* COUNT AS OVERLOADING
===============================================================================

Each of these is a compile-time error ("method is already defined"):

  1. Changing only the RETURN TYPE
         int    add(int a, int b)
         double add(int a, int b)                  // ERROR

  2. Changing only the parameter NAMES
         void show(int a)
         void show(int b)                          // ERROR

  3. Changing only the ACCESS MODIFIER
         public  void show(int a)
         private void show(int a)                  // ERROR

  4. Changing only STATIC / NON-STATIC
         static void show(int a)
         void        show(int a)                   // ERROR

  5. Changing only the THROWN EXCEPTIONS
         void show(int a) throws IOException
         void show(int a) throws SQLException      // ERROR

  6. Changing only FINAL / NON-FINAL on a parameter
         void show(int a)
         void show(final int a)                    // ERROR

The parameter LIST -- its types, its count, or the order of its types -- is
the only thing that creates an overload. Everything else is ignored.


===============================================================================
PART 9 : VARARGS OVERLOADING
===============================================================================

    void print(int a)     { }
    void print(int... a)  { }

    print(5);       // calls print(int)    -- fixed arity always beats varargs
    print(5, 6);    // calls print(int...)
    print();        // calls print(int...) with a zero-length array

  * The varargs parameter must be the LAST parameter in the list.
  * A method can have only ONE varargs parameter.
  * Inside the method, the varargs parameter is simply an array.
  * f(int...) and f(long...) together are ambiguous for the call f().


===============================================================================
PART 10 : OVERLOADING vs OVERRIDING -- SIDE BY SIDE
===============================================================================

                          OVERLOADING              OVERRIDING
    --------------------  -----------------------  -----------------------
    Polymorphism type     Compile-time / static    Runtime / dynamic
    Who decides           The compiler             The JVM
    Decided when          Compile time             Run time
    Method name           Same                     Same
    Parameter list        MUST differ              MUST be identical
    Return type           Ignored by the compiler  Same or covariant
    Access modifier       Anything                 Same or wider
    Exceptions            Anything                 Same or narrower
    Inheritance needed    No                       Yes -- mandatory
    Number of classes     Usually one              Always two or more
    Static methods        Can be overloaded        Cannot -- that is hiding
    Private methods       Can be overloaded        Cannot -- not inherited
    Final methods         Can be overloaded        Cannot
    Purpose               Caller convenience       Extensibility

One line each:

    OVERLOADING = same name, DIFFERENT parameters, SAME class, compiler decides.
    OVERRIDING  = same name, SAME parameters, PARENT and CHILD, JVM decides.


===============================================================================
PART 11 : CHECKLIST -- IS THIS A VALID OVERLOAD?
===============================================================================

    [ ] Same method name
    [ ] Parameter list differs by TYPE, COUNT, or ORDER of types
            (if the parameter list is identical it will NOT compile)
    [ ] Not relying on the return type alone to tell them apart
    [ ] Not relying on the access modifier alone
    [ ] Not relying on static / non-static alone
    [ ] Not relying on the thrown exceptions alone
    [ ] No two candidates equally good for any call you actually make
            (otherwise: "reference to X is ambiguous")
    [ ] Only ONE overload contains real logic; the rest delegate to it
    [ ] Each delegating overload ADDS information before passing it down


===============================================================================
PART 12 : TRAPS AND INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================================

  1. Passing null -- the MOST SPECIFIC type wins

         void show(String s)  { }
         void show(Object o)  { }
         show(null);          // calls show(String) -- String IS-A Object,
                              // so String is more specific

         void show(String s)         { }
         void show(StringBuilder s)  { }
         show(null);          // COMPILE ERROR -- unrelated types, neither
                              // one is more specific

  2. int vs Integer vs long

         void f(long x)     { }
         void f(Integer x)  { }
         f(10);             // calls f(long) -- Phase 2 beats Phase 3

  3. Can you overload main()?
         Yes. But the JVM only ever launches
         public static void main(String[] args). Any other main is an
         ordinary method you must call yourself.

  4. Can you overload a private / static / final method?
         Yes to all three. None of them can be OVERRIDDEN, which is a
         favourite way to test whether you have mixed up the two topics.

  5. Does overloading need inheritance?
         No. Overriding cannot work without it.

  6. Is constructor overloading the same thing?
         Yes -- identical rules. Combine it with this(...) for chaining.

  7. Why can't the return type alone distinguish two methods?
         Because a caller may ignore the return value entirely:
         obj.show();  -- the compiler would have no way to choose.

  8. All these terms mean the SAME thing, and interviewers switch freely:
         method overloading = compile-time polymorphism = static polymorphism
                            = static binding = early binding


===============================================================================
PART 13 : PRACTICE LIST -- WHAT TO CODE AFTER READING THIS
===============================================================================

  1. A Calculator class with add() overloaded 4 ways
     (int,int / double,double / three ints / int array).

  2. Force "reference to sum is ambiguous" on purpose, read the compiler
     message, then fix it. Errors you caused yourself stick.

  3. Prove the resolution order: write show(long), show(Integer) and
     show(int...) in one class, call show(10), and PREDICT before you run.

  4. Overload by changing only the ORDER of parameter types
     -- show(int, String) and show(String, int) -- and call both.

  5. Write a varargs sum(int... n) and call it with 0, 1 and 5 arguments.

  6. Add constructor overloading to any class in your Constructor package
     and chain the constructors with this(...).

  7. Take any class where you wrote the same logic in two overloads and
     refactor it so only one method holds the logic. Count the lines before
     and after.
